  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>Iran's Foreign Minister Araghchi confirmed on October 8 that Tehran had received Washington's formal counter-response to the seven-day Hormuz plan and was reviewing it, with a formal reply expected "within a few days." Qatar served as the channel for the document delivery; no text was made public. President Pezeshkian separately characterized the talks as "meaningless" in a statement to Iranian media the same day — a characterization consistent with hardline domestic pressure rather than a formal policy position, given Araghchi's simultaneous diplomatic framing carried higher institutional authority. Secretary Rubio described "progress" in the indirect channel during a Senate Foreign Relations Committee hearing on October 8. → moved <span class="cite">F8</span>, <span class="cite">F9</span></p>
      <p>Four of five major Israeli polling organizations now show Eisenkot's Yashar party holding a plurality over Likud, a reversal from the near-tie conditions registered when F5 was created on September 3. Channel 12 (Sep 22) put Yashar at 24 seats to Likud's 23; Channel 13 (Sep 29) had Yashar 22, Likud 18; Kan (Oct 5) polled Yashar 24, Likud 23; Maariv recorded a 21-21 tie; i24NEWS is the lone outlier with Likud at 27 and Yashar at 22. Prime minister preference polling by Channel 13 (Oct 2) showed Eisenkot at 48% to Netanyahu's 36%. The October 8 Avi Gil broadcast — featuring Eisenkot's accountability framing — received sustained prime-time coverage on Channels 12 and 13 without an effective Likud counter-broadcast. → moved <span class="cite">F5</span>, <span class="cite">F10</span></p>
      <p>The Wall Street Journal reported September 26 that Trump told senior aides he plans to launch fresh strikes on Iran after the November 3 midterms; CNBC confirmed October 8 that US CENTCOM has completed preparations for resumed operations with no order given. The White House declined to comment on the military readiness posture. Rubio's Senate testimony characterizing the indirect channel as showing "progress" ran concurrently with the CENTCOM confirmation — the two postures have coexisted in the same news cycle without an official reconciliation statement from the administration. → moved <span class="cite">F11</span></p>
      <p>Mojtaba Khamenei has not appeared publicly in any format authenticated by international monitors since approximately February 28, 2026 — more than seven months of absence from any verified recording. Iranian state media (Mehr, IRNA, Tasnim) have continued to circulate archival video; Mehr ran a third pre-war clip on October 7, drawn from a 2018 Basij ceremony, as Iran International noted in its monitoring dispatch the same day. The most recent publicly attributed content is a handwritten letter published in late August without accompanying video or audio. President Pezeshkian claimed a 7-8 hour meeting with the Leader in late September, but the statement carried no independent verification and IRIB produced no authenticated post-war footage. → moved <span class="cite">F12</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 4. SCENARIO MAP ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="scenario-map">
      <div class="prob-bar-container">
        <div class="prob-bar prob-escalation" style="width:33%;">Escalation 33%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-status" style="width:50%;">Protracted 50%</div>
        <div class="prob-bar prob-deescalation" style="width:17%;">De-esc 17%</div>
      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (33%):</strong> Trump announces resumed large-scale Iran strikes in the Nov 3–10 window (F11, p=0.35) absent any ceasefire framework (F9, p=0.07); IRGC mine and vessel-attack activity intensifies rather than halts before the post-midterm window opens.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (50%):</strong> No deal and no resumed large-scale strikes through November; both sides remain in the current standoff-with-indirect-talks posture, Hormuz traffic depressed, US midterm domestic politics constraining offensive action and Iranian hardliners blocking a substantive diplomatic concession.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (17%):</strong> F8 resolves YES (Iran formal response before Oct 12) catalyzes a substantive back-channel negotiation and a rapid F9-equivalent bilateral framework before Oct 17; Hormuz traffic recovery begins before end of October.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ========== 5. INSIDE IRAN ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media framing between October 5–9 held to a dual narrative: IRGC operational announcements dominated IRIB primetime, while Araghchi's October 8 remark on the counter-proposal received truncated coverage in Tasnim and IRNA without editorial context or follow-up. Kayhan ran a lead editorial on October 7 describing any formal response to the American counter-proposal as a potential "capitulation to Zionist pressure," directly addressing the diplomatic question that Araghchi left technically open. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span></p>
      <p>Mehr News published a third archival video clip of Mojtaba on October 7, drawn from a 2018 Basij ceremony. Iran International flagged the publication as consistent with a documented regime pattern of using pre-war footage to manage leadership visibility gaps. No IRIB scheduling announcement referenced a planned Mojtaba appearance, and no authenticated post-war footage has been produced by any outlet subject to independent international verification per monitoring services as of October 9. → supports <span class="cite">F12</span></p>
      <p>Press TV published a statement on October 8 attributed to a "senior security official" describing the US counter-proposal as containing "new and unacceptable conditions" — the first state-media acknowledgment that a formal US response document existed. Tasnim simultaneously ran a counter-piece quoting a "Foreign Ministry source" describing "constructive elements" in the American position. The divergence — SNSC-sourced rejection versus MFA-sourced receptivity — ran in state media simultaneously for at least 24 hours without a regime-level resolution statement. → supports <span class="cite">F8</span>, <span class="cite">F9</span></p>
      <p>The Iranian rial hit a record low of approximately 950,000 rials to the dollar on October 8 per open-market exchange rates (Bonbast.com) cited by BBC Persian. IRIB primetime ran a counter-narrative segment attributing the currency deterioration to "enemy economic warfare." The Central Bank of Iran issued a statement on October 9 without acknowledging the exchange rate. Fuel rationing in Tehran, Mashhad, and Isfahan tightened on October 7, with the government attributing the shortfall to "infrastructure maintenance" — a formulation used for infrastructure damage in past reporting cycles. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

